# HTTP

Value types and helpers for serving the web. A handler is an ordinary function
from a `Request` to a `Response`; the `Serve` statement runs it once per request.
Route by matching on the request's path; read headers with `headerOf` and query
parameters with `queryOf`; build a reply with the constructors below and add
headers with `withHeader`.

## Definition

## Note
An HTTP request as a handler sees it: the method, the percent-decoded path, the
body text, the headers (names lowercased by the wire parser), and the
percent-decoded query parameters.

A Request has:
    a method, which is Text.
    a path, which is Text.
    a body, which is Text.
    a headers, which is Map of Text to Text.
    a query, which is Map of Text to Text.

## Note
What went wrong with a request the page sent: the `status` the server answered, and a `message`
to show. A status of `0` means nothing answered at all.

An HttpError has:
    a status, which is Int.
    a message, which is Text.

## Note
An HTTP response a handler returns: status, content type, body, and extra headers.

An HTTP response a handler returns: a numeric status, a content type, a text
body, an optional raw byte body sent instead of the text when non-empty, and
extra headers (set them through `withHeader`, which enforces the hop-by-hop
denylist and the response-splitting guard).

A Response has:
    a status, which is Int.
    a contentType, which is Text.
    a body, which is Text.
    a bytes, which is Seq of Int.
    a headers, which is Map of Text to Text.

## Note
A 200 OK response with a plain-text body.

## To plain (body: Text) -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 200 and contentType "text/plain" and body body.

## Note
A 200 OK response with an HTML body.

## To html (body: Text) -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 200 and contentType "text/html" and body body.

## Note
A 200 OK response with a JSON body.

## To json (body: Text) -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 200 and contentType "application/json" and body body.

## Note
A 404 Not Found response.

## To notFound () -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 404 and contentType "text/plain" and body "Not Found".

## Note
401 Unauthorized — "I do not know who you are", so the visitor should sign in.

`forbidden` is the OTHER refusal, 403: "I know who you are, and no", which signing in again will
not help. A guard that conflated the two would send an authenticated visitor to a login page they
are already past, forever.

## To unauthorized () -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 401 and contentType "text/plain" and body "Unauthorized".

## Note
403 Forbidden — the request is understood and refused. See `unauthorized` for the other one.

## To forbidden () -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 403 and contentType "text/plain" and body "Forbidden".

## Note
A 302 redirect to `where`, carrying the `Location` header that says so.

That header is the whole point: a redirect that does not name where it is going is a dead end —
the browser is told to move and not where, so it stays put and the visitor sees nothing happen.

Use `permanentRedirectTo` only when the move really is permanent: a 301 tells the browser AND every
cache in between to remember it, which is close to irreversible.

## To redirectTo (where: Text) -> Response:
    Let r be a new Response with status 302 and contentType "text/plain" and body "".
    Inspect withHeader(r, "location", where):
        When Ok (out):
            Return out.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Response with status 500 and contentType "text/plain" and body "bad redirect target".

## Note
A 301 permanent redirect. See `redirectTo` — this one is remembered by caches.

## To permanentRedirectTo (where: Text) -> Response:
    Let r be redirectTo(where).
    Return a new Response with status 301 and contentType r's contentType and body r's body and headers r's headers.

## Note
A `Set-Cookie` value with the safe attributes already on it.

They are on by default because a cookie is how a session survives a request, and the caller who
most needs them is the least likely to know to ask:

- `HttpOnly` keeps a script from reading it, which is what makes an XSS a nuisance rather than a
  full account takeover.
- `SameSite=Lax` is what a CSRF defence rests on — a cookie the browser will not attach to a
  cross-site request cannot be replayed by another site's form.
- `Secure` keeps it off plaintext HTTP, where anyone on the path can read it.
- `Path=/` makes it the whole site's cookie rather than one directory's.

A name or value carrying `;`, a newline or a quote is REFUSED (answers `""`): those end the cookie
— or the HEADER — and everything after becomes something the server never wrote. That is response
splitting, and a session cookie is exactly where it would be aimed. Refusing beats sanitising: a
silently-truncated session id is a bug nobody can see.

## To cookie (name: Text) and (value: Text) -> Text:
    Return cookieFor(name, value, cookiesAreSecure()).

## Note
The same cookie, saying for itself whether it is `Secure`.

A `Secure` cookie is never sent back over plain HTTP, so a session cannot survive one hop on an
address that is not HTTPS. `localhost` is not the case that bites — browsers treat it as a
trustworthy origin and do send `Secure` cookies to it. The one that bites is the LAN address,
`http://192.168.x.x`, which is how anybody tests a page on their phone: there the browser accepts
the `Set-Cookie` and then declines to send it, which looks exactly like a login that silently does
not log anybody in.

Dropping `Secure` drops ONLY `Secure`. `HttpOnly` and `SameSite` defend against different attacks
and have nothing to do with the transport.

## To cookieFor (name: Text) and (value: Text) and (secure: Bool) -> Text:
    If isCookieSafe(name) is equal to false:
        Return "".
    If isCookieSafe(value) is equal to false:
        Return "".
    If name is equal to "":
        Return "".
    Let base be name + "=" + value + "; Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax".
    If secure is equal to true:
        Return base + "; Secure".
    Return base.

## Note
Whether cookies this program sets are marked `Secure`. They are, unless `LOGOS_COOKIE_INSECURE` is
set to something.

The default does not move, because the two failure modes are not symmetrical: a missing `Secure` in
production is a session token travelling in clear text on every request, and an unwanted one in
development is a login that does not stick. Only the second is noticed immediately, and only the
first is a breach — so opting OUT is a decision somebody makes on purpose, and opting IN is one
they forget.

## To cookiesAreSecure () -> Bool:
    Let mutable asked be "".
    Inspect get("LOGOS_COOKIE_INSECURE"):
        When Some (v):
            Set asked to v.
        When None:
            Set asked to "".
    If asked is equal to "":
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
The cookie that DELETES one: the same name, an empty value, and a zero lifetime.

Setting it is the only way to say "forget this" — a logout that could not would leave the session
cookie sitting in the browser.

## To expiredCookie (name: Text) -> Text:
    If isCookieSafe(name) is equal to false:
        Return "".
    If name is equal to "":
        Return "".
    Let base be name + "=; Path=/; Max-Age=0; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax".
    If cookiesAreSecure() is equal to true:
        Return base + "; Secure".
    Return base.

## Note
Whether `t` may appear in a cookie name or value: no `;` (it ends the cookie), no CR or LF (they
end the HEADER), no `"`, no `,` and no space.

## To isCookieSafe (t: Text) -> Bool:
    Repeat for bt in text_bytes(t):
        If bt is equal to 59:
            Return false.
        If bt is equal to 44:
            Return false.
        If bt is equal to 34:
            Return false.
        If bt is equal to 32:
            Return false.
        If bt is less than 32:
            Return false.
        If bt is equal to 127:
            Return false.
    Return true.

## Note
`response` with `name=value` set as a cookie, safe attributes and all.

## To withCookie (response: Response) and (name: Text) and (value: Text) -> Response:
    Let c be cookie(name, value).
    If c is equal to "":
        Return response.
    Inspect withHeader(response, "set-cookie", c):
        When Ok (out):
            Return out.
        Otherwise:
            Return response.

## Note
The value of cookie `name` the request carries, or `""` when it sent none.

A `Cookie` header is a `; `-separated LIST — a browser sends every cookie for the site at once — so
a server that only understood one would break the moment anything else set one.

## To cookieOf (request: Request) and (name: Text) -> Text:
    Let raw be headerOf(request, "cookie").
    If raw is equal to "":
        Return "".
    Repeat for part in split(raw, ";"):
        Let piece be trim(part).
        Let at be indexOf(piece, "=").
        If at is greater than 0:
            If textUpTo(piece, at - 1) is equal to name:
                Return textFrom(piece, at).
    Return "".

## Note
A 200 OK response carrying raw bytes (e.g. `bytesResponse("application/wasm", readBytes("app.wasm"))`).

## To bytesResponse (contentType: Text) and (payload: Seq of Int) -> Response:
    Return a new Response with status 200 and contentType contentType and body "" and bytes payload.

## Note
Reads request header `name` case-insensitively (HTTP header names are case-insensitive by RFC), returning `""` when absent.

An empty header value also reads as `""` — `hasHeader` tells them apart. When
two keys of a hand-built map differ only by case, the first in map order wins.
## To headerOf (request: Request) and (name: Text) -> Text:
    Return headerIn(request's headers, name).

## Note
The value of header `name` in a header MAP, case-insensitively, or `""` when it carries none.

A request's headers and a response's headers are the same shape and are read the same way. The
router asks this of a RESPONSE — to find the `Set-Cookie` a route set — and `headerOf` asks it of a
REQUEST. One walk, so the two can never disagree about what counts as a match, which is exactly the
kind of drift a second hand-written case-folding loop invites.

## To headerIn (headers: Map of Text to Text) and (name: Text) -> Text:
    Let want be lower(name).
    Repeat for (k, v) in headers:
        If lower(k) is equal to want:
            Return v.
    Return "".

## Note
Reports whether request header `name` is present, case-insensitively —
distinguishing an absent header from one whose value is empty.

## To hasHeader (request: Request) and (name: Text) -> Bool:
    Let want be lower(name).
    Let hs be request's headers.
    Repeat for (k, v) in hs:
        If lower(k) is equal to want:
            Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
Reads query parameter `name` (already percent-decoded by the wire parser), returning `""` when absent.

Query keys are data, not protocol tokens, so the lookup is case-sensitive;
`hasQuery` distinguishes absent from empty.
## To queryOf (request: Request) and (name: Text) -> Text:
    Let q be request's query.
    If q contains name:
        Return item name of q.
    Return "".

## Note
Reports whether query parameter `name` is present (case-sensitively) —
distinguishing an absent parameter from one whose value is empty.

## To hasQuery (request: Request) and (name: Text) -> Bool:
    Let q be request's query.
    If q contains name:
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
Reports whether byte `bt` may appear in a header name: the RFC 7230 token set,
ALPHA / DIGIT / ! # $ % & ' * + - . ^ _ ` | ~.

## To isHeaderTokenByte (bt: Int) -> Bool:
    If bt is at least 48:
        If bt is at most 57:
            Return true.
    If bt is at least 65:
        If bt is at most 90:
            Return true.
    If bt is at least 97:
        If bt is at most 122:
            Return true.
    If bt is equal to 33:
        Return true.
    If bt is at least 35:
        If bt is at most 39:
            Return true.
    If bt is equal to 42:
        Return true.
    If bt is equal to 43:
        Return true.
    If bt is equal to 45:
        Return true.
    If bt is equal to 46:
        Return true.
    If bt is at least 94:
        If bt is at most 96:
            Return true.
    If bt is equal to 124:
        Return true.
    If bt is equal to 126:
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
Reports whether `name` is a valid header name: non-empty and made only of
RFC 7230 token bytes (so a CR, LF, space, or colon can never reach the wire).

## To isHeaderName (name: Text) -> Bool:
    Let bs be text_bytes(name).
    If length of bs is equal to 0:
        Return false.
    Repeat for bt in bs:
        If isHeaderTokenByte(bt) is equal to false:
            Return false.
    Return true.

## Note
Reports whether `value` is safe as a header value: no CR, LF, or NUL byte —
the response-splitting guard.

## To isHeaderValue (value: Text) -> Bool:
    Let bs be text_bytes(value).
    Repeat for bt in bs:
        If bt is equal to 13:
            Return false.
        If bt is equal to 10:
            Return false.
        If bt is equal to 0:
            Return false.
    Return true.

## Note
Reports whether lowercased header name `key` is hop-by-hop framing the wire
writer owns: connection, content-length, transfer-encoding, keep-alive, upgrade.

## To isHopByHop (key: Text) -> Bool:
    If key is equal to "connection":
        Return true.
    If key is equal to "content-length":
        Return true.
    If key is equal to "transfer-encoding":
        Return true.
    If key is equal to "keep-alive":
        Return true.
    If key is equal to "upgrade":
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
Returns a copy of `response` with header `name` set to `value`; the input is never mutated.

Names are stored lowercased, so re-setting the same name in any casing overwrites — one logical header, one wire line.
Rejects with an `Err`, never a silent drop: a non-token name, a hop-by-hop name
(the wire owns those), content-type (set the `contentType` field instead), and
any CR/LF/NUL in the value (response splitting).
## To withHeader (response: Response) and (name: Text) and (value: Text) -> Result of Response and Text:
    If isHeaderName(name) is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "withHeader: header name is not a valid token".
    Let key be lower(name).
    If isHopByHop(key) is equal to true:
        Return a new Err with error "withHeader: {key} is hop-by-hop; the wire owns it".
    If key is equal to "content-type":
        Return a new Err with error "withHeader: set contentType on the Response instead of a content-type header".
    If isHeaderValue(value) is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "withHeader: header value contains a forbidden control byte".
    Let mutable fresh be a new Map of Text to Text.
    Let hs be response's headers.
    Repeat for (k, v) in hs:
        Set item k of fresh to v.
    Set item key of fresh to value.
    Let s be response's status.
    Let ct be response's contentType.
    Let b be response's body.
    Let bs be response's bytes.
    Let out be a new Response with status s and contentType ct and body b and bytes bs and headers fresh.
    Return a new Ok with value out.
